 This keeps the issuer, discovery document, JWKS URL, and token endpoint internally consistent for local JWT validation while supporting LocalStack-style confidential clients and resource-server-backed scopes.
+
+### Refresh grants and rotation
+
+`POST /cognito-idp/oauth2/token` also accepts `grant_type=refresh_token`, `client_id` and
+`refresh_token`. Confidential clients must provide their secret using `client_secret` or
+HTTP Basic authentication. Either active secret is accepted during client-secret rotation;
+after removing a secret, the remaining active secret is still required.
+Custom domains expose the same operation at `/oauth2/token`
+and bind it to their user pool. The client must allow the authorization code flow.
+
+OAuth refresh and `GetTokensFromRefreshToken` share token validation and rotation. They
+preserve the original granted scopes, subject, issuer, client and token-family `origin_jti`.
+Adding scopes to a client's configuration never grants them to an existing refresh token;
+removed scopes are dropped. OAuth returns an ID token only for a grant containing `openid`.
+Access and ID token lifetimes follow the client's configured validity values.
+
+With `RefreshTokenRotation.Feature=ENABLED`, each refresh returns a distinct refresh token
+with the original absolute expiry. The submitted token remains usable for its configured
+`RetryGracePeriodSeconds` (0 through 60 seconds) after first use; retries do not extend that
+deadline. `RevokeToken` revokes the family, including rotated descendants. The legacy
+`REFRESH_TOKEN_AUTH` and `REFRESH_TOKEN` authentication flows are refused when rotation is
+enabled. With rotation disabled, refresh omits a new refresh token and the original remains
+usable until expiry or revocation.
+
+Granted scopes, absolute expiry and rotation deadlines use Cognito's configured storage
+backend in `cognito-refresh-tokens.json`. They survive persistent storage reloads and are
+removed on reset or deletion of their user, client or pool. Expired and consumed records
+are cleaned up when tokens are issued or refreshed. Signed tokens from older Floci versions
+remain usable for nonrotating API refresh with their original API scope; OAuth refresh and
+rotation require the persisted grant created by the current version.
+
+Missing OAuth refresh tokens return `invalid_request`; invalid, expired, revoked or
+cross-client tokens and disabled or deleted users return `invalid_grant`. Client credential
+failures return `invalid_client`, and clients without the code flow return
+`unauthorized_client`.
